ntered workspace layout creates unnecessary margins on wider screens. This implies a need to adjust the workspace's sizing and alignment logic to eliminate the empty gutter. Further investigation into the column's defined widths may be required.\n\n\nThe hard-cod\n<truncated 3571 bytes>\nhe existing stepper functionality. Further investigation is needed to determine the location and behavior of the removed controls.\n\n\nThe new tab navigation and modified controls indicate a possible restructuring of user interactions and feature organization. The removed animation and speed controls require locating their new positions or understanding if functionality was consolidated. It's likely the previous action button functionality is now integrated into a tabbed menu. I'll need to further investigate these changes.\n\n\nVerified element lookups in JavaScript files do not depend on the specific placement of `stepProgressLabel` within `.brand-area`. The `dsa_dashboard.js` and `app.js` code uses element IDs and class names, not specific parent-child relationships within the DOM.\n\nAnalyzed the `dsa_dashboard.js:showDsa()` function. This hides and shows elements depending on the mode of the visualizer. Identified that the elements are hidden by setting `display: none` using inline styles. Verified element visibility is managed independently from specific parent elements and that the tab system won't conflict with these settings.\n\n\nExamined the behavior of the `.top-bar-status-area` when its child elements are hidden. Determined that the layout handles the zero-width state of the status area, ensuring the `.brand-area` expands to fill the remaining space without layout artifacts.\n\nAnalyzing the contents of `.area-variables` in the HTML reveals sections for stack frames and heap memory visualization.  The structure provides labels, containers, and elements likely used for displaying scope and memory data.\n\n\nThe `.area-variables` element requires vertical extension within the layout to fill available space, with the issue arising from fixed height settings. A sticky positioning and calculated `min-height` property, along with a flex layout, will allow the element to expand vertically. A footer element within `.area-variables` can provide memory model insights, using `margin-top: auto` to position it at the bottom.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html\"","EndLine":"55","StartLine":"15","toolAction":"\"Viewing index.html lines 15-55\"","toolSummary":"\"View index.html lines 15 to 55\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
